(function(){
var additionalData = [
["external.html?file=../NintendoSwitch-EventExhibitionGuide-2_0-en.html","Nintendo Switch Event Exhibition Guide",
"Nintendo Switch Event Exhibition Guide\n \t Introduction\n \t Equipment Used\n \t Hardware\n \t Software\n \t Notes for Event Operation\n \t Ensuring the Safety of Attendees\n \t Exhibition Method\n \t Hardware Management\n \t Handling of Specifications Different From Retail Products\n \t Controller Signal Interference\n \t Signal Interference in Local Communication\n \t Malfunctions of the IR Motion Camera\n \t Screen Brightness\n \t Attachment: Checklist\n \t Revision History\nIntroduction\nThis document describes the requirements and recommendations for using development tools such as EDEV Kits at demo events for end users.\nWhen creating applications for exhibition, comply with the Nintendo Switch Guidelines, which is available online.\nIf you are uncertain about how to handle an issue, contact the Nintendo Technical Support Department.\nEquipment Used\nHardware\nDevelopment Hardware That Can Be Used\nThe only development hardware systems that can be used for events are EDEV, HDEV, and ADEV. SDEV cannot be used.\nFor equipment other than the system itself, such as Joy-Con controllers, the Nintendo Switch dock, the AC adapter, the Pro Controller, and HDMI cables, a version equivalent to the commercial version is included for all accessories other than the Pro Controller. Use these accessories.\nUse a commercially available Pro Controller.\nDevelopment Hardware That Cannot Be Used\nSome of the earlier EDEV Kits contain black Joy-Con controllers that are different from the production version. Do not use the black Joy-Con at events for the general public.\n Figure 1 Joy-Con\nSoftware\nSDK and Firmware Used\nUse the SDK described as acceptable for master submissions and the firmware versions that require testing, described in Technical Information > Master ROM Submission Information (https://developer.nintendo.com/group/development/g1kr9vj6/tech-info/master-submission-information) on the Nintendo Developer Portal.\nTesting\nThe ROM data for exhibition at events does not need to be submitted for Lotcheck.\nUse the same firmware you would use at your actual event to test the operations from either the exhibition mode or the HOME Menu.\nInstallation Target\nTo prevent theft, avoid starting software from a game card or microSD card, and start it from the system memory instead.\nNotes for Event Operation\nThis section describes the requirements and other important information to consider for event operation.\nEnsuring the Safety of Attendees\nWhen exhibiting applications that use the accelerometer and gyroscope to detect movement, make sure to provide Joy-Con wrist straps. In addition, increase the safety of players by leaving sufficient distance between players and other attendees or equipment.\nExhibition Method\nTo prevent any operational problems at demo events, make sure to fully test the specifications and exhibition method of an application prior to the event.\nFor example, if you want users to try both TV mode and handheld mode, consider an exhibition method where users can touch the console.\nHardware Management\n \tManage hardware carefully during exhibitions.\n \tFor exhibitions that span multiple days, transfer and store the equipment in a locked location each day after the exhibition period for that day ends.\nHandling of Specifications Different From Retail Products\nPhotographing Development Hardware\nDo not allow users to take photos of the back of development hardware (EDEV, HDEV, and ADEV).\nShowing Screens Other Than the Game Screen\nAt the exhibition venue, do not allow attendees to view or operate screens other than the game screen, particularly developer features such as DevMenu.\nEither use the exhibition mode feature designed for events, or delete DevMenuApplication if you are using the HOME Menu.\nWe recommend using exhibition mode if there is no need to switch applications.\n Info\n When handling issues via DevMenu, remove the system from the dock and perform operations in handheld mode so video is not displayed on the TV.\nUser Accounts\nThe icon for a user account created via DevMenu cannot be selected when user accounts are displayed with the HOME Menu. For this reason, create accounts intended for events from the HOME Menu.\n Figure 2 User Icon Created via DevMenu (Use Prohibited)\nExhibition Mode\nDevMenu includes an exhibition mode to support various features that are necessary at events. In exhibition mode, the functionality of the system changes as follows.\n \tThe HOME Button is disabled and the system does not transition to DevMenu.\n \tAlthough images are saved when the Capture Button is pressed, the overlay notification is not displayed.\n \tWhen the system is turned on, the specified application starts automatically from DevMenu.\n \tWhen resuming from sleep mode, the application resumes without displaying DevMenu.\nIn addition, you can use the following convenient features for exhibition events.\n \tPlay timer feature to automatically close an application after a specified amount of time.\n \tFeature to use save data you prepared specifically to be a starting point for the application.\nFor more information about exhibition mode, see NintendoSDK Documents > Features > Controlling the Development Hardware > DevMenu > Exhibition Features.\nJapanese: https://developer.nintendo.com/html/online-docs/g1kr9vj6-ja/Packages/SDK/NintendoSDK/Documents/Package/contents/Pages/Page_522465110.html\nEnglish: https://developer.nintendo.com/html/online-docs/g1kr9vj6-en/Packages/SDK/NintendoSDK/Documents/Package/contents/Pages/Page_522465110.html\nEnabling Exhibition Mode\n \tFrom DevMenu, select Debug.\n \tIn Launch Mode Settings, change Exhibition Mode to Enabled.\n \tThe \"Reboot device to reflect changes\" message is displayed. Tap the POWER Button at the upper right to restart the system.\nWhen first enabling exhibition mode, restart DevMenu because the application to start automatically has not been configured. You must select the application to start.\nSelecting the Application to Start\n \tGo to Application, and press the Y Button to select the application to start automatically.\n \tThe application that was set starts automatically after restarting the system.\nYou can only set the automatic startup application in DevMenu with exhibition mode enabled. You cannot set this option when exhibition mode is disabled, and you cannot start it from the DevMenu application.\n Info\n If the application prompts you to select a user at startup, you must create a user account in advance.\nCreate a user account from the HOME Menu. (As described previously, do not use user accounts created with DevMenu.)\n Info\n If the application set to start automatically is deleted, application startup fails when the system is turned on, and continues to try to restart. In this case, open DevMenu as described below in Starting DevMenu in Exhibition Mode, and change the application that is automatically started.\nDisabling Exhibition Mode\n \tFrom DevMenu, select Debug.\n \tIn Launch Mode Settings, change Exhibition Mode to Disabled.\n \tThe \"Reboot device to reflect changes\" message is displayed. Tap the POWER Button at the upper right to restart the system.\nStarting DevMenu in Exhibition Mode\nWith the Joy-Con controllers attached to the console, repeatedly press ZL + R + Down simultaneously immediately after starting the system to transition to DevMenu.\nNote: The Down Button (\n) is the button pointing downwards when the left Joy-Con is attached to the console.\nHOME Menu\nSwitching to the HOME Menu\nIf you set Initial Boot Menu to Home Menu, the screen at startup changes to the HOME Menu.\n \tFrom DevMenu, select Debug.\n \tIn Launch Mode Settings, set Initial Boot Menu to Home Menu.\n \tThe \"Reboot device to reflect changes\" message is displayed. Tap the POWER Button at the upper right to restart the system.\nDeleting the DevMenu Application\nTo prevent the DevMenuApp icon from displaying on the HOME Menu, delete the DevMenuApp icon using the following procedure.\n \tFrom the HOME Menu, go to DevMenu Application and press the + Button.\n \tFrom Data Management, select Delete Software.\n \tIn the dialog box that appears, select Delete.\nStarting DevMenu From the HOME Menu With the DevMenu Application Deleted\nYou can start DevMenu from the HOME Menu with the DevMenu Application deleted.\n \tCheck that all applications have been closed.\n \tAt the lower right of the HOME Menu, select the sleep icon.\n \tWhile the screen indicating that the console is about to enter sleep mode is displayed, press the L or R stick simultaneously with the + or - Button.\nIf pressing the buttons simultaneously is difficult, first press and hold the L or R Stick, and then press the + or - Button (or do the reverse).\n \tDevMenu starts.\nTo return to the HOME Menu, use the Power Button at the upper right to restart the console.\nReusing the DevMenu Application\nThis section describes how to make the DevMenu Application available again after deleting it from the HOME Menu.\nInstalling NSP Files\nThis method reinstalls the DevMenu Application while keeping the other installed applications as is.\nFor more information, see Installing and Running Applications in the Nintendo Switch EDEV Usage Manual, the Nintendo Switch HDEV Usage Manual, or the Nintendo Switch ADEV Usage Manual.\nA PC or a microSD card containing the NSP file is required.\nThe DevMenu Application NSP file is located in NintendoSDK\\TargetTools\\NX-NXFP2-a64\\DevMenuApp\\Release.\nRunning Initialize EDEV (or Initialize HDEV or Initialize ADEV)\nIf you run the Initialize EDEV process to initialize the development hardware, the DevMenu application will be installed again. Note that installed applications will be deleted.\nFor more information, see Nintendo Switch EDEV Usage Manual > EDEV Operations > Initializing the EDEV, Nintendo Switch HDEV Usage Manual > HDEV Operations > Initializing the HDEV, or Nintendo Switch ADEV Usage Manual > ADEV Operations > Initializing the ADEV.\nA PC is required.\nController Signal Interference\nWhen using wireless controllers such as the Joy-Con, there can be interference from Wi-Fi spots and other wireless devices placed in the venue. Issues can occur, such as difficulty connecting the controller, input not being applied in the game, or disconnecting from the console.\nYou can use the following methods to alleviate these issues.\n \tMove the console and controllers closer together.\nThe most effective way to resolve interference with surrounding equipment is to move the devices that need to communicate closer together. We recommend having the console and controller within two meters of each other, and within one meter if possible.\n \tWhen multiple Switch consoles are used in the exhibition, make sure there is sufficient space between them.\nAs an estimate, consider making the distance between consoles greater than the distance between a console and its controllers. We recommend leaving a distance of at least one meter between consoles.\n \tConnect the minimum number of required controllers.\nThe risk of wireless interference increases with each controller connected wirelessly to the same console. Connections are relatively stable if you limit the number of controllers connected wirelessly to 4 or fewer.\n \tMove the consoles farther away from signal sources.\nIf there are many people in the exhibition hall, there can be interference with signals from wireless devices carried by attendees. You might be able to alleviate this problem by moving the console farther away from crowded locations such as the lines where guests are waiting to play the game.\n \tConnect Pro Controllers with wired connections.\nPro Controllers can transmit data via the USB charging cable.\nConversely, even if you attach Joy-Con controllers to the Joy-Con charging grip and connect the grip to the console via a USB cable, data is not transmitted through the wired connection.\nConnecting Pro Controllers via Wired Connection\n \tUse a USB charging cable to connect the Pro Controller to a Nintendo Switch dock that is connected with an AC adapter.\n \tFrom the HOME Menu:\tSelect System Settings.\n\tSelect Controllers and Sensors > Pro Controller Wired Communication, and use the confirm button to switch the option from OFF to ON.\n \tFrom DevMenu:\tSelect Controller.\n\tSelect the Enable Pro Controller USB Communication check box.\n Info\n NFC features are unavailable while using a wired connection. When using NFC features such as reading amiibo, disconnect the USB charging cable and use wireless communication.\nSignal Interference in Local Communication\nWhen using local communication, signal interference from Wi-Fi spots and other wireless devices can occur, just as with wireless controllers. Signal interference can cause issues such as difficulty connecting, operations on one device being difficult to apply on another device, or disconnecting.\nYou can use the following methods to alleviate these issues.\n \tMove the consoles closer together.\nMove the consoles participating in local communication closer together.\n \tUse handheld mode.\nLocal communication also interferes with wireless controllers, so using the controllers attached to the console in handheld mode might reduce interference.\n \tMove the consoles farther away from signal sources.\nSimilar to the solution for wireless controller signal interference, you might be able to alleviate the problems by moving the consoles away from crowded areas such as the lines where guests are waiting to play the game.\nMalfunctions of the IR Motion Camera\nWhen exhibiting applications that use the IR Motion Camera on the right Joy-Con, the device might be affected by lighting equipment in the exhibition hall due to the characteristics of infrared hardware, and it might not operate correctly.\nConsider countermeasures such as instructing users to face the bottom of the right Joy-Con towards an area without light sources, such as a wall. (Confirm this solution before the exhibition.)\nScreen Brightness\nIn general, turn the brightness all the way up and turn off Auto-Brightness. Make adjustments depending on the surrounding environment at the event.\nBe careful when Auto-Brightness is on, because the screen might become dark when the console is held at certain angles. For ADEV, the light sensor is located at the top of the console.\nAttachment: Checklist\n Note: This checklist does not need to be submitted to Nintendo. Use it when needed for verification.\n \tNumber \tVerification Item \tConfirmed\n \tHardware Preparation\n \t1 \t The hardware used is EDEV, HDEV, or ADEV. (SDEV is not used.)\n \t\n \t2 \tA commercially available Pro Controller is used. \t\n \t3 \tNo black Joy-Con is used. \t\n \tSoftware Preparation\n \t4 \tThe SDK version used is specified as valid for master submission, and the firmware version is the version required for testing. \t\n \t5 \tThe firmware for testing is the same as for the event, and operation is verified via exhibition mode or the HOME Menu. \t\n \t6 \tThe application is installed to the system NAND. \t\n \tOperation\n \t7 \tApplications that require significant movement are placed at an appropriate distance so that attendees can play safely. \t\n \t8 \tThere is a locked area for managing hardware, ROM cards, and other equipment. \t\n \t9 \tExhibition mode or the HOME Menu is used to prevent screens other than the game screen from being displayed. \t\n \t10 \tIf the HOME Menu is used, the DevMenu application has been deleted. \t\nRevision History\n \tVersion \tDate \tOverview\n \t2.0 \t2021-12-16 \tAdded descriptions for ADEV.\n \t1.0 \t2020-12-17 \tInitial version.\n© 2020-2021 Nintendo. All rights reserved.\nAll company and product names in this document are the trademarks or registered trademarks of their respective companies.\n\tNintendo Confidential\n\tThe content of this document is highly confidential and should be handled accordingly.\nThe contents of this document cannot be duplicated, copied, reprinted, transferred, distributed, or loaned, in whole or in part, without the prior approval of Nintendo.\nThis document contains confidential and proprietary information of Nintendo, and is protected under confidentiality agreements as well as the intellectual property laws of the United States and of other countries.\nNo part of this document may be released, distributed, transmitted, or reproduced in any form, including by any electronic or mechanical means and by including within information storage and retrieval systems, without written permission from Nintendo.\nCONFIDENTIAL\n",1,"en-US"],
];
for (var x = 0; x < additionalData.length; x++) Search.indexData.push(additionalData[x]);
Search.additionalData = additionalData;
})();
Search.enableApiSearch = true;
